module

public import Silmaril.Project.CompilesTo.Defs
public import Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C.Defs

/-!
# Standard problem shapes: C functions, segment by segment

The segment-level statements of `Silmaril.Project.CompilesTo` as shapes, for rows generated by
`tools/c-tv` (`docs/projects/compiles-to.md`), next to the function-level C shapes of
`Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C`. A row fixes the model `I` (`Silmaril.X86.Open.isa`), the function's
correspondence `F : FnData` (its `CFn`, its bytes and its program points), the typed Ail of the
C file and the function's C name.

| name | statement |
|---|---|
| `silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.point` | `PointOf F k line`: point `k`'s Core fragment is the Core of the statement at `line` (or the procedure body, or its return label) (C ↔ Core, one segment) |
| `silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.segment` | `SegmentSim I F k`: the segment at point `k` is simulated (Core ↔ x86, one segment) |
| `silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.segment_from_c` | `SegmentFromC I ail name F k line`: `c.elaborates_to` for `F.cfn`, the point, and the segment (C ↔ x86, one segment) |
| `silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.canonical_entry` | `CompilesToCanonical M p frame F`: `c.compiles_to` with the C function read from one canonical entry state per argument tuple (weaker; see below) |

`SegmentSim` assumes the source cannot go wrong and terminates (`Source.Safe`): undefined
behaviour of the C function permits any machine behaviour, and that is part of the statement,
not an omission. The function-level row is `c.compiles_to`
(`Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C.CompilesTo`); `Thms` proves it from the segment rows, the
placement's `Enterable`, and the C function's safety on some argument tuple
(`FnData.compilesTo_of_segments`).

## Two readings of the C function

`CFn.meaningFrom F E` is the meaning of `F` read from the entry states `E a`, each with the
empty caller continuation: safe on `a` when every run from every `E a` state is safe, and `r` a
result when some run from some `E a` state returns it.

* **Every entry** (`c.compiles_to`): `CFn.meaning F = F.meaningFrom (fun a s =>
  F.CoreEntry [] (argValues a) s)` (`CFn.meaning_eq`): every memory and environment that
  satisfies the verified Core library's invariant for the parameters. A call from any program
  state is covered. Its non-vacuity (`F.meaning.Inhabited`) asks for safety from every such
  state, which needs a proof over all memories.
* **One canonical entry** (`c.compiles_to.canonical_entry`): `F.canonicalMeaning =
  F.meaningFrom (fun a s => s = F.canonicalEntry a)`: the parameters allocated in order from an
  empty memory (`canonicalMem`), the environment binding them and nothing else. Its non-vacuity
  is the safety of one state, which a forced run checked by evaluation proves. **It does not
  cover** a call from any other state: another memory (other objects, another allocator
  frontier, other object ids or addresses), another environment, or a caller continuation. For
  the profile of the C shapes (`int` parameters, no memory beyond them, no calls, no pointer
  comparison) a run cannot observe these, but no lemma here proves that; until one does, a
  canonical row says less than a `c.compiles_to` row. `C.CompilesTo.of_canonical` (in `Thms`) is
  exactly the missing step: a canonical row and `F.meaning.Inhabited` give the standard row.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Silmaril.Project.Shapes.CompilesTo

open Silmaril.Project.CompilesTo
open Silmaril.Cerberus
open Silmaril.Cerberus.Core
open Silmaril.Cerberus.Core.Dynamics
open Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C (CFn argValues ValueIs ElaboratesTo)
open Silmaril.Project.Shapes.SysV (Meaning)
open Silmaril.X86.SystemV.CApi (Signature Arguments)

/-! ## Segments -/

/-- What a point's fragment is, as C ↔ Core: the procedure body (entry), the Core of the
statement at `line` (statement boundary), or the return label's body with its parameter
(closing brace). -/
def PointFaithful (F : FnData) (k : Nat) (line : Int) : Prop :=
  ∃ p, F.points[k]? = some p ∧
    match p.kind with
    | .entry => p.frag = F.body
    | .slots cont _ => StmtAt F.body line p.frag ∧ cont = stmtCont F.body line
    | .ret sym => RetAt F.body sym p.frag

/-- **C ↔ Core, one segment**: point `k`'s Core fragment is the Core of its source segment. -/
@[problem "silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.point"
  (title := "A segment's Core fragment is the Core of its C statement")]
def PointOf (F : FnData) (k : Nat) (line : Int) : Prop := PointFaithful F k line

/-- **Core ↔ x86, one segment**: the segment at point `k` is simulated on `I`. -/
@[problem "silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.segment"
  (title := "A Core segment and its machine code segment correspond")]
def SegmentSim (I : Model) (F : FnData) (k : Nat) : Prop := F.Segment I k

/-- **C ↔ x86, one segment**: the C function elaborates to `F`'s Core (`c.elaborates_to`), point
`k` is the Core of its statement, and the segment is simulated. -/
@[problem "silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.segment_from_c"
  (title := "A C statement compiles to its machine code segment")]
def SegmentFromC (I : Model) (ail : Ail.TypedProgram) (name : String) (F : FnData) (k : Nat)
    (line : Int) : Prop :=
  ElaboratesTo ail name F.cfn ∧ PointFaithful F k line ∧ F.Segment I k

/-! ## The meaning of a C function from given entry states -/

/-- **The meaning of `F` read from the entry states `E a`** (each with the empty caller
continuation, as `CFn.meaning`): safe on `a` when every run from every `E a` state is safe;
`r` is a result when some run from some `E a` state returns it. -/
def _root_.Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C.CFn.meaningFrom {sig : Signature} (F : CFn sig)
    (E : Arguments sig.arguments → State → Prop) : Meaning sig where
  Safe a := ∀ s, E a s → (F.source []).Safe s
  Returns a r := ∃ s t v, E a s ∧ Relation.ReflTransGen (F.source []).step s t ∧
    (F.source []).final t v ∧ ValueIs sig.result v r

/-- The Core entry states with the arguments `a` and the empty caller continuation: the entry
states of `CFn.meaning`. -/
def _root_.Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C.CFn.Entry {sig : Signature} (F : CFn sig)
    (a : Arguments sig.arguments) (s : State) : Prop :=
  F.CoreEntry [] (argValues a) s

/-! ## The canonical entry -/

/-- The argument objects of the canonical entry for `n` arguments: ids `0, 1, …`, each `int` four
bytes below the previous one, from `65536`. -/
def canonicalObjs (n : Nat) : List (Int × Int) :=
  (List.range n).map fun i => ((i : Int), 65536 - 4 * ((i : Int) + 1))

/-- The memory of the canonical entry: the argument values allocated in order (`argAlloc`) from
an empty memory whose allocator starts at `65536`. -/
def canonicalMem (ns : List Int) : Mem.Concrete.State :=
  ns.foldl argAlloc { lastAddress := 65536 }

/-- **The canonical entry state** of `F` with the arguments `a`: the procedure body in control,
the parameters bound to the canonical argument objects (and nothing else bound), the procedure
frame alone on the stack. -/
def _root_.Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C.CFn.canonicalEntry {sig : Signature} (F : CFn sig)
    (a : Arguments sig.arguments) : State :=
  ⟨.effectExpr F.body
      (extendEnv ((F.params.map (·.1)).zip
        ((canonicalObjs F.params.length).map fun o =>
          (.object (.pointer (.allocated o.1 o.2)) : Val))) ∅),
    [.procedureReturn F.bodyLabels], canonicalMem (argValues a)⟩

/-- **The meaning of `F` from its canonical entry** (one state per argument tuple). Weaker than
`CFn.meaning`; see the module docstring. -/
def _root_.Silmaril.Project.Shapes.C.CFn.canonicalMeaning {sig : Signature} (F : CFn sig) :
    Meaning sig :=
  F.meaningFrom fun a s => s = F.canonicalEntry a

/-- **The placed machine function implements the C function `F` from its canonical entry**
(`SysV.Implements` against `F.canonicalMeaning`). Weaker than `c.compiles_to`: it does not
cover calls from any other program state (another memory, allocator state, environment or
caller continuation), and its non-vacuity is the safety of the canonical entry only. With
`F.meaning.Inhabited` it gives the `c.compiles_to` row (`C.CompilesTo.of_canonical`). -/
@[problem "silmaril.shapes.c.compiles_to.canonical_entry"
  (title := "A placed x86-64 function implements a C function from its canonical entry")]
def CompilesToCanonical {sig : Signature} (M : Model) (p : Placement) (frame : Nat)
    (F : CFn sig) : Prop :=
  Silmaril.Project.Shapes.SysV.Implements M p sig frame F.canonicalMeaning

end Silmaril.Project.Shapes.CompilesTo

end

